Now bug #2: `DateTime#plus works across the 100 barrier when passing through February`. Changelog 1.11.1: "Fix DateTime#plus() when spanning across AD 100". The bug is in util.js — likely `objToLocalTS`:

```js
export function objToLocalTS(obj) {
  let d = Date.UTC(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day, obj.hour, obj.minute, obj.second, obj.millisecond);

  // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
  if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
    d = new Date(d);
    d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
  }
  return +d;
}
```

The bug: when the date is e.g. year 100, month 1, day 1... hmm. Actually the issue: Date.UTC(100, ...) gives year 100 correctly (only 0-99 are remapped). But the fix: when year is, say, 99 and month is 12 → Date.UTC(99, 11, 31) → year 1999-12-31, then setUTCFullYear(1999-1900=99) → year 0099-12-31. Fine.

Hmm, where's the bug "across the 100 barrier when passing through February"? Let's think: DateTime.plus({years: ...}) or plus({months}) uses daysInMonth / objToLocalTS. Actually the fix in luxon for 1.11.1 was:

```js
